WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.399
Film Courage: I’ve been reading this book
called LOL [LOL: From Homeless to Multimillion-dollar

00:00:02.399 --> 00:00:04.029
Global Business Leader] The writer is Christy
Dreiling.

00:00:04.029 --> 00:00:08.750
It’s about her life growing up impoverished
and then she became a multi-million dollar

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:13.009
salesperson and vice-president of a company.

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:18.250
She talked about what she learned was the
poverty mentality versus people who break

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:22.750
out of that, which [comes from] a willingness
to find a solution.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:26.900
That people who stay in a poverty mentality,
don’t [find a solution] and those who break

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.490
out, they find solutions.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.390
They have a problem, they find a solution.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:37.500
That being said, people creating opportunities
for themselves, in Los Angeles as a writer.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.630
It doesn’t even have to be in LA.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:43.480
But those who don’t and say “I don’t
know what to do?

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.690
I’ve been submitting [and nothing].”

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:49.710
Whatever, versus those who find a way out
of that problem.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:50.710
What have you noticed?

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:53.670
Kathie Fong Yoneda: Well I think what you
are saying is true.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:57.920
Let’s face it, we can’t have it all!

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.010
We can’t be perfect in every single area.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:07.460
And I think one of the things you were talking
about earlier, we were talking about how there

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.800
are differences in how things are done in
the industry now.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:19.290
And one of the things is there are a lot more
people who are in career counseling as consultants.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:31.120
And sometimes when you get stuck and especially
with writers are so internal about how they

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:38.300
process things and sometimes it’s hard to
be external and get things out there and to

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.110
do it the right way.

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:45.370
I know that there are people who are career
counselors who have really helped in that

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:48.850
area.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:55.830
They talk to you and try to find out what
it is about you that is special and what is

00:01:55.830 --> 00:02:00.150
it about your writing that is meaningful and
they try to bring that out of you and for

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:04.920
you to try to use that and to help you with
your work.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.770
And I think that this is something that writers
can look into.

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:14.100
Not just writers, but I know actors and actresses
have to go to career coaches to help them

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.170
out with that.

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:20.800
You would think with actors and actresses
that they would be very external and very

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:22.470
‘out there.’

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:28.100
But a lot of actors and actresses that I’ve
met, there’s a lot of self-doubt sometimes

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:29.370
behind them.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:34.730
And so every once and a while, they get stuck
and they sometimes go to career coaches to

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:36.530
help them out.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:41.410
So there are career coaches who are specialists
in terms of writing.

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:46.970
Film Courage: Let’s say somebody at this
point can’t afford that.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:49.940
What are some other things people can do to
create an opportunity for themselves?

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.230
Sounds like a writing group or any type of
creative group?

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:55.000
Kathie Fong Yoneda: A writing group is wonderful.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:01.780
The more people you communicate with who are
doing the same thing you are doing means more

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:07.160
opportunities because I’ve noticed that
if one person finds a little bit of success,

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.560
they are willing to share it.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:15.680
Like if they can get an agent or if they get
a producer to read their material, they are

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.300
then ready to say “Oh?

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:22.750
Well, maybe then I can help this person who
has a project that maybe that producer is

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:23.750
looking for.”

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:27.950
As I mentioned earlier, that’s what happened
to one of my clients when she was in a writer’s

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:28.950
group.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:30.910
So I think that really does help.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:41.380
I also think that if you go to different (in
terms of networking) networking groups that

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:42.850
they have here in LA.

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:49.610
There is one that is the first Friday of each
month and I think it is called First Friday

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:50.610
or something?

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:55.470
But they have a group where people get together
and they will say we are meeting at such and

00:03:55.470 --> 00:04:01.420
such (and usually it’s a cocktail bar or
some group or restaurant).

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:04.701
And a lot of times there are other writers
there.

00:04:04.701 --> 00:04:10.670
There are also other writers who have made
it, who were in that group who sometimes come.

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:13.750
There are things like that.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:18.190
Somebody else I know said that when they first
moved here, they knew no one.

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:24.490
What they did was they Googled “Screenwriter
Meetup” and they came up with a list of

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:27.400
things and she went to every single one of
those.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:32.620
She formed her own writer’s group from the
people who were of interest to her that she

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:34.580
felt she made a connection with.

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:36.770
She now has a lot of friends.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:39.099
She came knowing no one.

00:04:39.099 --> 00:04:41.330
She’s from North Dakota.

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:46.090
And she said there was nobody in her town.

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:48.650
She put up a little thing saying “Any screenwriters?”

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:52.550
She put it up at the local University in her
town.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:57.969
And there is nobody there who said anything
about “Yeah, they are interested in screenwriting.”

00:04:57.969 --> 00:05:01.939
So she said “I guess I need to move to California.”

00:05:01.939 --> 00:05:04.379
And so she just took a leap of faith and she
did that.

00:05:04.379 --> 00:05:09.069
I mean it doesn’t work for everybody but
it sure worked for her.

00:05:09.069 --> 00:05:13.669
She now has an agent, she’s been doing punch-ups.

00:05:13.669 --> 00:05:17.310
She’s a great comedy writer.

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:24.300
And so she’s been doing punch-ups and she’s
been doing some stuff for late night comedy

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.669
work to do writing there.

00:05:26.669 --> 00:05:34.559
It’s kind of interesting to see how people
start to make slow progress just by the connections

00:05:34.559 --> 00:05:35.559
that they are making.

00:05:35.559 --> 00:05:40.319
Film Courage: I think we all know someone
who has great intentions but they just kind

00:05:40.319 --> 00:05:44.669
of wait for emails, they wait by the phone
and they don’t do anything about it.

00:05:44.669 --> 00:05:49.969
What have you seen in that sort of type that
gets them out where they can stop waiting

00:05:49.969 --> 00:05:53.919
and relying on someone else and making opportunities.

00:05:53.919 --> 00:05:57.419
Like the woman who started her own Meetup
group, which is an excellent idea.

00:05:57.419 --> 00:05:58.919
But that’s kind of scary?

00:05:58.919 --> 00:06:02.830
That takes a leap of…I mean I get it, she
knew some of the people [previously] but that’s

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:03.830
intimidating?

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:04.830
What do you see?

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:09.979
Kathie Fong Yoneda: She told me the most intimidating
thing was moving from North Dakota to California.

00:06:09.979 --> 00:06:11.999
Film Courage: Sure, that’s scary in itself.

00:06:11.999 --> 00:06:14.539
Kathie Fong Yoneda: It was scary, but she
told me if she could do that, she had nothing

00:06:14.539 --> 00:06:15.539
to lose.

00:06:15.539 --> 00:06:20.749
She looked at it like when she went to these
Meetup groups, she knew nobody.

00:06:20.749 --> 00:06:23.759
She had nothing to lose and everything to
gain.

00:06:23.759 --> 00:06:27.629
And I think a lot of it is your attitude when
you come in.

00:06:27.629 --> 00:06:33.409
If you are looking for something and you really
want it, you make the effort to let somebody

00:06:33.409 --> 00:06:34.789
get to know you better.

00:06:34.789 --> 00:06:37.599
And it does, it takes a huge leap of faith.

00:06:37.599 --> 00:06:42.740
But it’s worthwhile in the long run for
people who do put themselves out there.

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:48.309
It’s not easy but you have to do that.

00:06:48.309 --> 00:06:50.969
There are a lot of people who just give up.

00:06:50.969 --> 00:06:58.729
And if they give up, to me it means it wasn’t
meant for you.

00:06:58.729 --> 00:07:04.460
But if you stick with it, something is eventually
going to happen.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:09.909
Like I told you, I interviewed over 200 of
these people who are professional writers.

00:07:09.909 --> 00:07:12.469
The average was 8 scripts.

00:07:12.469 --> 00:07:16.939
There was one guy who is now on staff on television.

00:07:16.939 --> 00:07:19.490
He’s a really good television writer.

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:21.639
And I asked him “Well, gosh?

00:07:21.639 --> 00:07:22.639
How does it feel?”

00:07:22.639 --> 00:07:25.460
You know how many scripts it took him?

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:27.969
It took him 12 scripts.

00:07:27.969 --> 00:07:32.599
And I said “Well what did you do when you
reached 8 scripts?”

00:07:32.599 --> 00:07:34.929
And he said “I don’t know.

00:07:34.929 --> 00:07:38.330
There was just something about…I could not
stop.

00:07:38.330 --> 00:07:42.259
It became like “I’m going to do this”
And he just had to do it.

00:07:42.259 --> 00:07:48.729
And he kept writing and writing and he finally
got to that 12th script and I said “Gee,

00:07:48.729 --> 00:07:50.451
I’d like to read some of those first 10
scripts.”

00:07:50.451 --> 00:07:54.509
And he said “No…I don’t think you want
to.”

00:07:54.509 --> 00:07:57.699
And I said “Yeah, but I’d like to see
what they were like.”

00:07:57.699 --> 00:08:01.229
And he said “You know what, I don’t let
anybody read them.”

00:08:01.229 --> 00:08:03.039
And I said “Well do you still have them?”

00:08:03.039 --> 00:08:06.309
And he said “Yeah, I have them in a drawer
at the bottom of my desk.”

00:08:06.309 --> 00:08:08.249
And I said “Well, why are you keeping them
then?”

00:08:08.249 --> 00:08:10.319
And he said “It’s to keep me humble.

00:08:10.319 --> 00:08:11.849
Because how could I even think?

00:08:11.849 --> 00:08:16.979
I look at those 10 and say how could I even
think I was a writer when I read those 10

00:08:16.979 --> 00:08:18.029
scripts?”

00:08:18.029 --> 00:08:20.699
I mean it just amazed me!

00:08:20.699 --> 00:08:23.879
And this guy is quite successful.

00:08:23.879 --> 00:08:29.430
I mean, he makes 6 figures a year as a writer
and he’s doing quite well, but it took him

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:30.430
that long.

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:35.900
And so you have to have that determination,
you just have to have to do it.

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:39.479
You can’t just say “Oh, well.

00:08:39.479 --> 00:08:42.770
It’s not going to happen.”

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:45.930
It’s like…I always tell people…it’s
like you’re knocking at Hollywood’s door.

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:52.510
They ain’t going to answer…you are not
going to open that door if you don’t give

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:56.020
them the answers or if you don’t tell them
what it is that you want.

00:08:56.020 --> 00:08:59.770
And it’s not just what you want, you have
to show them what you have.

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:02.350
So want and have are two different things.

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:06.410
You know, you can dream about it, but you
have to make the dream come true.

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:11.610
Dreaming about it and not doing anything about
it, that’s the difference between success

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:13.310
and failure.

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:17.460
And some of the people who I’ve seen that
I’ve known for years who are doing quite

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:22.370
well in the business, it’s because they
deserved it, they really stuck to it.

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:24.050
They just couldn’t help themselves.

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:25.800
They just had to keep writing.

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:30.360
And they believed in themselves that they
said “I’ve just got to do this.”

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:34.370
A lot of times they had to take two or three
jobs in order to do it.

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:41.260
A lot of times family and marriage came and
went in between all of that because I asked

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:45.310
one of them one time…his wife was a teacher
and they had twins and so he had to be the

00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:49.340
one to stay home with the twins, which is
not easy.

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:54.950
And I asked him “How did you do it and get
your assignments in on time?”

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:58.890
And he said he basically had to stay up at
night.

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:02.930
He would set the alarm, he would go to sleep
at 10 o’clock, he would get up at 3 o’clock

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:08.660
in the morning, do the feeding, and then he
would work for three hours straight.

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:14.450
And he gave himself that he would have to
write so many words or how many scenes or

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:16.490
whatever it is and that’s how he did it.

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:18.720
He just stuck to it.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:22.560
And he said there were times where he would
just sit there and he would be waiting and

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:26.650
waiting for those words to just come and they
wouldn’t.

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:31.240
And he finally just started writing down just
random words and pretty soon (he said) he

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:32.540
would just come across something.

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:34.370
It was like “Oh, wait a minute?

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:35.740
That word.”

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:39.010
And pretty soon it would start to flow.

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:44.340
And he said he doesn’t understand it to
this day, he just knows it happened.

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:50.810
And he said that’s what he did but he always
made sure even in those times when he felt

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:56.430
like he couldn’t do it, if he wasn’t going
to write he was going to sit there and stare

00:10:56.430 --> 00:11:01.630
at the page and just write random words until
something came to him.

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:07.300
Because he felt he had to at least psychically
be doing it, or psychically typing it up for

00:11:07.300 --> 00:11:14.220
him to be able to feel that “Okay, even
if I am not sitting here with an idea, I’m

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:18.480
just going to keep typing until something
strikes me.”

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:23.950
That’s literally how he said he got some
of his assignments done.

00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:26.240
Some of it was trash, you know.

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:29.560
He would type for an hour and nothing would
come up and then all of the sudden something

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:30.700
would click.

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:36.650
So I think he just felt that he had to do
it, he forced himself to do it.

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:41.070
It worked for him and he had the weirdest
hours but it worked for him.

